  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe optimization task is to find a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085, subject to h being in [0,1] and its integral being 1. The problem is non-convex due to quadratic terms in the cross-correlation, making it prone to local minima. Our strategy leverages convex relaxation by linearizing the quadratic constraints iteratively, allowing us to use efficient convex optimization techniques. The approach systematically approximates the non-convex objective, potentially leading to a better global optimum.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe aim to minimize:\n$$ C_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] (1 - h[(i + k) \\% n]) \\right) $$\nsubject to:\n$$ 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\quad \\forall i $$\n$$ \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] = \\frac{n}{2} $$\n\nThe cross-correlation for each shift k is linearized around the current iterate h, transforming the non-convex problem into a series of convex subproblems. This allows us to leverage convex optimization solvers for each step.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   i. Seed the random number generator for reproducibility.\n   ii. If initial_h_values exist, interpolate to n_points and normalize.\n   iii. Otherwise, initialize h to a uniform distribution scaled to sum(n/2).\n\nb. Optimization loop:\n   i. For each iteration:\n      - Compute the maximum cross-correlation shift k.\n      - Linearize the cross-correlation constraint around current h.\n      - Set up and solve a convex optimization problem with linearized constraints and original feasibility constraints.\n      - Update h with the solution.\n      - Project back into [0,1] and ensure sum(h) = n/2 if necessary.\n      - Track the best solution found.\n\nc. Termination:\n   i. Stop when the time budget is exhausted or no improvement is detected.\n   ii. Return the best feasible solution found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points=100 balances accuracy and computational efficiency.\n- Use linearization for quadratic terms to form convex subproblems.\n- Apply convex solver for each step, ensuring constraint satisfaction.\n- Consider multiple initializations or restarts if time permits.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach convex iteration processes constraints in O(n\u00b2), with 1000 iterations expected within 600s. The algorithm includes safeguards to handle numerical instability and ensures feasible solutions at each step.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks verify h values and sum constraints. The best solution is validated against the evaluator to ensure correctness. The program returns the best h, its C\u2085, and n_points.",
